---
source: crates/standalone/fixture/src/lib.rs
---
Serve a built deploy the way production does.

Usage: ink serve [OPTIONS] [FOLDER]

Examples
  # Serve .ink/out of this folder on 127.0.0.1:8080
  ink serve

  # Serve .ink/out of another folder
  ink -C garden serve

  # Self-host: serve, watch and build git sites from disk,
  # restarting a crashed loop three times
  ink serve --data state --git-scheme file --restart-limit 3

  # Turn on the API and send sign-in links by SMTP
  ink serve --data state --mail smtp --mail-from ink@example.org --smtp-host mail.example.org

Arguments
  [FOLDER]  A folder `ink build` wrote into, or with
            `--host-mode folders` a folder of them, one per
            host name [default: .ink/out of the site]

Server
      --addr <ADDRESS>
        The address to listen on [default: 127.0.0.1:8080]
      --host-mode <HOST_MODE>
        How a request's host picks a deploy [default: auto]
      --data <DATA>
        Turns on the control plane and its API. Its
        database, blobs, sealing key and the operator's
        public keys live in this folder. Without it only the
        folder is served
      --api-host <API_HOST>
        The host name whose requests are API calls, signed
        with a key (`ink login`) [default: api.localhost]
      --origin <ORIGIN>
        Where sites are served, for the addresses the API
        reports (`<handle>.<host>`) and the origin passkeys
        are made for: give the address a browser uses to
        reach this server [default: `http://localhost:` and
        the listening port]

Limits
      --api-body <API_BODY>
        The largest JSON request body, in bytes.
        [default: 65536]
      --upload-bytes <UPLOAD_BYTES>
        The largest uploaded archive, in bytes.
        [default: 268435456]
      --upload-files <UPLOAD_FILES>
        The most files in an uploaded archive.
        [default: 10000]
      --upload-file <UPLOAD_FILE>
        The largest file in an uploaded archive, in bytes.
        [default: 67108864]
      --upload-tree <UPLOAD_TREE>
        The largest tree `ink push` may send, in bytes; the
        tree is read whole. [default: 8388608]
      --upload-part <UPLOAD_PART>
        The most bytes of files in one `ink push` request,
        held in memory while it is checked and stored.
        [default: 67108864]
      --export-bytes <EXPORT_BYTES>
        The largest exported archive, in bytes.
        [default: 268435456]
      --poll-interval <POLL_INTERVAL>
        Seconds between polls of a git source. At least 10.
        [default: 600]
      --poll-max <POLL_MAX>
        Seconds a source that does not change is backed off
        to. [default: 3600]
      --abuse-limit <ABUSE_LIMIT>
        Abuse reports that may be sent per source, as
        `<count>/<window>`. Reports take no account, so this
        is what bounds them. [default: 5/1h]
        [env: INK_ABUSE_LIMIT]
      --enrol-limit <ENROL_LIMIT>
        Asks to enrol a key that may be sent per source, as
        `<count>/<window>`. They take no account, so this
        keeps one source from filling the queue of waiting
        keys. [default: 5/1h] [env: INK_ENROL_LIMIT]
      --handle-hold <DAYS>
        Days a handle is held after its site left it, so no
        one else can claim it; a verified owner's handle is
        held for good. [default: 365] [env: INK_HANDLE_HOLD]
      --limit-stored <BYTES>
        Bytes an account may store across its sites; a
        deploy over it is refused whole.
        [default: 1073741824] [env: INK_LIMIT_STORED]
      --limit-builds <N>
        Builds an account may start in 24 hours; a request
        over it is refused. [default: 60]
        [env: INK_LIMIT_BUILDS]
      --limit-sites <N>
        Sites an account may have. [default: 10]
        [env: INK_LIMIT_SITES]
      --limit-deploys <N>
        Deploys of a site that are kept, the live one
        included; older ones are removed with the files only
        they used. [default: 20] [env: INK_LIMIT_DEPLOYS]
      --remove-step <N>
        The most blobs one step of removing a site deletes.
        A removal runs in steps this size, so a site with
        many files never holds a request or a worker for
        long. [default: 500] [env: INK_REMOVE_STEP]
      --preview-days <DAYS>
        Days a preview of a branch is served before it ends
        and is swept. Previews are public to anyone with the
        address. [default: 7] [env: INK_PREVIEW_DAYS]
      --preview-branches <N>
        The most branches of one public git site the watch
        builds as previews, by name; `0` builds none. The
        branch the site itself builds is not one of them.
        [default: 10] [env: INK_PREVIEW_BRANCHES]
      --limit-domains <N>
        The most custom domains one site has, proved and
        not. A claim of a name costs a row, so the number
        bounds what one site can ask for. [default: 10]
        [env: INK_LIMIT_DOMAINS]
      --limit-reports <N>
        The most abuse reports open at once, over every
        source. Anyone may send one, so the number bounds
        what many sources together can store; a report the
        operator actioned by suspending a site stops
        counting, and raising this is the only other way to
        make room. [default: 10000] [env: INK_LIMIT_REPORTS]
      --blob-grace <SECONDS>
        Seconds a blob that no deploy and no preview names
        stays before it is deleted. A build that skipped
        uploading a blob because the store held it keeps it
        until its deploy is recorded, so this must outlast
        that gap. [default: 900] [env: INK_BLOB_GRACE]
      --domain-recheck-days <DAYS>
        Days between two reads of the TXT record that proves
        a custom domain. A name whose record no longer
        carries its token stops resolving at once.
        [default: 30] [env: INK_DOMAIN_RECHECK_DAYS]
      --domain-lapse-days <DAYS>
        Days a custom domain that failed its check keeps its
        name before any site may claim it. The owner may
        publish the record again in that time and the name
        serves again. [default: 14]
        [env: INK_DOMAIN_LAPSE_DAYS]
      --limit-build-time <SECONDS>
        Seconds one build may take: a worker holds a request
        for no longer than this, so a build that runs past
        it is given to another. [default: 600]
        [env: INK_LIMIT_BUILD_TIME]

Mail
      --mail <MAIL>      Where sign-in mail goes
                         [default: stdout] [env: INK_MAIL]
      --mail-from <MAIL_FROM>
        The address mail is sent from. Required with `--mail
        smtp` [env: INK_MAIL_FROM]
      --smtp-host <SMTP_HOST>
        The SMTP server's host name [env: INK_SMTP_HOST]
      --smtp-port <SMTP_PORT>
        The SMTP server's port. [default: 587, or 465 with
        `--smtp-security implicit`] [env: INK_SMTP_PORT]
      --smtp-security <SMTP_SECURITY>
        How the SMTP connection is secured
        [default: starttls] [env: INK_SMTP_SECURITY]
      --smtp-user <SMTP_USER>
        The SMTP username [env: INK_SMTP_USER]
      --smtp-password-file <SMTP_PASSWORD_FILE>
        A file holding the SMTP password.
        `INK_SMTP_PASSWORD` holds it instead; there is no
        flag for the password itself, which a process list
        would show [env: INK_SMTP_PASSWORD_FILE]
      --login-base <LOGIN_BASE>
        Where the link in a message points; the token is
        added as one more path segment. [default: the
        dashboard's `/login` on the API's address]
        [env: INK_LOGIN_BASE]
      --outbox <OUTBOX>  How many messages wait to be sent
                         before more are dropped.
                         [default: 256] [env: INK_OUTBOX]
      --courier-rest <COURIER_REST>
        How long the mail sender rests when nothing waits.
        [default: 1s] [env: INK_COURIER_REST]

Sign-in
      --login-get-signs-in
        Whether opening the link with a GET signs in, which
        a mail scanner can do for you. Without it a GET only
        looks, and a page in front of the API POSTs
        [env: INK_LOGIN_GET_SIGNS_IN]
      --login-ttl <LOGIN_TTL>
        How long a sign-in link works. [default: 15m]
        [env: INK_LOGIN_TTL]
      --admin-ttl <ADMIN_TTL>
        How long the first-run link works. [default: 24h]
        [env: INK_ADMIN_TTL]
      --session-expiry <SESSION_EXPIRY>
        How a session ends [default: sliding]
        [env: INK_SESSION_EXPIRY]
      --session-idle <SESSION_IDLE>
        How long a sliding session may sit unused.
        [default: 7d] [env: INK_SESSION_IDLE]
      --session-limit <SESSION_LIMIT>
        The longest a session lasts. [default: 30d]
        [env: INK_SESSION_LIMIT]
      --signup <SIGNUP>
        Who may make an account by signing in: `first` (the
        first, by the first-run link), `open` (any address
        that receives mail) or `closed`. [default: first]
        [env: INK_SIGNUP]
      --rate-address <RATE_ADDRESS>
        Requests for a link per address, as
        `<count>/<window>`. [default: 5/1h]
        [env: INK_RATE_ADDRESS]
      --rate-source <RATE_SOURCE>
        Requests for a link per source (an IPv4 address or
        an IPv6 /64). [default: 20/1h]
        [env: INK_RATE_SOURCE]
      --rate-tracked <RATE_TRACKED>
        How many addresses and sources the limits remember
        at once. [default: 10000] [env: INK_RATE_TRACKED]
      --cookie-name <COOKIE_NAME>
        The session cookie's name. [default:
        `__Host-ink-session`, or `ink-session` on plain
        http] [env: INK_COOKIE_NAME]
      --cookie-unprefixed
        Take a `--cookie-name` that does not start with
        `__Host-` on a `Secure` cookie. Without it such a
        name is refused, because a `__Host-` name is what
        stops a sibling site from setting the cookie
        [env: INK_COOKIE_UNPREFIXED]
      --cookie-path <COOKIE_PATH>
        The session cookie's path. [default: /]
        [env: INK_COOKIE_PATH]
      --rp-id <RP_ID>
        The relying party id of passkeys: a host name the
        dashboard is served on or above. A passkey made
        under one id never works under another.
        [default: the API host] [env: INK_RP_ID]
      --rp-origin <RP_ORIGIN>
        An origin the dashboard is served from, which
        passkey ceremonies must come from. Repeat for more
        than one. [default: the origin of the API host]
        [env: INK_RP_ORIGIN]
      --allow-origin <ALLOW_ORIGIN>
        An origin besides the API's own that may post
        state-changing requests with a session cookie, such
        as a dashboard served on another host. Repeat for
        more than one [env: INK_ALLOW_ORIGIN]
      --challenge-limit <CHALLENGE_LIMIT>
        Passkey sign-ins that may start per source, as
        `<count>/<window>`. [default: 60/1h]
        [env: INK_CHALLENGE_LIMIT]
      --recovery-limit <RECOVERY_LIMIT>
        Recovery codes that may be tried per source, as
        `<count>/<window>`. [default: 10/1h]
        [env: INK_RECOVERY_LIMIT]
      --step-up-window <STEP_UP_WINDOW>
        How recent a passkey sign-in must be to add or
        remove a passkey, approve a key or make recovery
        codes. [default: 5m] [env: INK_STEP_UP_WINDOW]
      --forwarded-hops <FORWARDED_HOPS>
        How many reverse proxies of yours sit in front of
        the server. With it, the client's address is read
        from `X-Forwarded-For`, that many entries from the
        end, and a request without it is refused.
        [default: off, the connection's address is used]
        [env: INK_FORWARDED_HOPS]

Keys
      --audience <AUDIENCE>
        The authority a signature is made for, as a client
        writes it: `host[:port]` of the API. Not the `Host`
        header, so a proxy cannot choose it.
        [default: `--api-host` and the port of `--addr`]
        [env: INK_AUDIENCE]
      --auth-skew <AUTH_SKEW>
        Seconds either side of the server's time a signed
        request may be, and the quiet after a restart. At
        most 300. [default: 60] [env: INK_AUTH_SKEW]
      --auth-nonces <AUTH_NONCES>
        How many request nonces are remembered for one key
        at once. [default: 1024] [env: INK_AUTH_NONCES]
      --key-device <KEY_DEVICE>
        How long a device key lives. [default: 180d]
        [env: INK_KEY_DEVICE]
      --key-deploy <KEY_DEPLOY>
        How long a deploy key lives. [default: 90d]
        [env: INK_KEY_DEPLOY]
      --key-ceiling <KEY_CEILING>
        The longest a key may be asked to live.
        [default: 365d] [env: INK_KEY_CEILING]
      --enrol-ttl <ENROL_TTL>
        How long a person has to approve a key.
        [default: 10m] [env: INK_ENROL_TTL]
      --enrol-guesses <ENROL_GUESSES>
        How many wrong codes close enrolling. [default: 5]
        [env: INK_ENROL_GUESSES]
      --enrol-waiting <ENROL_WAITING>
        How many keys may wait for approval at once.
        [default: 20] [env: INK_ENROL_WAITING]
      --enrol-closed <ENROL_CLOSED>
        How long enrolling stays closed. [default: 1m]
        [env: INK_ENROL_CLOSED]

Hosting
      --site-ttl <SITE_TTL>
        Seconds a site's live deploy is remembered before
        the control plane is asked again: a new deploy or a
        rollback shows within this long. The default asks
        every time, since the lookup and the control plane
        share one process and one database, so there is no
        staleness to trade for speed; raise it only to spare
        the database a read per request. [default: 0]
      --site-deploys <SITE_DEPLOYS>
        How many decoded deploys the lookup keeps.
        [default: 64]
      --poll-lookout <POLL_LOOKOUT>
        Seconds before the watch looks again for a site it
        has not seen move, at most; so a new site is first
        polled within this long. At least 1. [default: 5]
      --worker-lease <WORKER_LEASE>
        Seconds a worker holds a build request before the
        queue gives it to another. [default: 900]
      --worker-idle <WORKER_IDLE>
        Seconds a worker rests when the queue is empty.
        [default: 2]
      --restart-limit <RESTART_LIMIT>
        How many times in a row the watch or the worker may
        crash before it is left stopped. [default: 5]
      --restart-delay <RESTART_DELAY>
        Seconds to wait before a crashed loop is started
        again. [default: 2]
      --restart-healthy <RESTART_HEALTHY>
        Seconds a loop must run for a crash after it to
        count as the first again. [default: 60]
      --git-scheme <GIT_SCHEMES>
        A kind of git address a site may be added with and
        the builder fetches; repeat for more.
        [default: https]
      --git-cache <GIT_CACHE>
        Where clones of git sites are kept.
        [default: <data>/git]
      --git-time <GIT_TIME>
        Seconds a fetch and read of a git source may take.
        [default: 120]
      --git-connect <GIT_CONNECT>
        Seconds to wait for a connection to an https git
        remote. [default: 10]
      --git-request <GIT_REQUEST>
        Seconds one https request to a git remote may take
        in all, its body included: also the longest a pack
        may take to arrive. [default: 120]
      --git-redirects <GIT_REDIRECTS>
        Which redirects of an https git remote are followed;
        one that leaves https never is. [default: first]
      --git-ca <GIT_CAS>
        A PEM file of certificate authorities to trust
        beside the bundled web roots, for a forge with a
        private authority; repeat for more. Certificates are
        always verified
      --resolver <RESOLVER>
        How the TXT records that prove a custom domain are
        read: `system` asks nameservers, `offline` asks
        none, so a claim is made and a proof says why it
        cannot be checked. [default: system]
        [env: INK_RESOLVER]
      --resolver-server <ADDR>
        A nameserver to ask, as `ADDRESS` or `ADDRESS:PORT`,
        in order; repeat for more. [default: the
        `nameserver` lines of `--resolver-file`]
        [env: INK_RESOLVER_SERVERS]
      --resolver-confirm <ADDR>
        A second set of nameservers, as `ADDRESS` or
        `ADDRESS:PORT`; repeat for more. When it is set, a
        proof record is trusted only when both sets give the
        same records, so one forged answer on the way to one
        nameserver proves nothing. DNSSEC is not checked
        either way. [default: none]
        [env: INK_RESOLVER_CONFIRM]
      --resolver-file <PATH>
        The file the system lists its nameservers in.
        [default: /etc/resolv.conf] [env: INK_RESOLVER_FILE]
      --resolver-timeout <SECONDS>
        Seconds one lookup of a proof record may take in
        all, shared by the nameservers in turn. 1 to 60.
        [default: 5] [env: INK_RESOLVER_TIMEOUT]
      --forge-hooks
        Take webhooks from forges at `POST
        /v1/hooks/{github,forgejo,gitlab}`, and let a site
        claim and prove its repository, which the server
        reads from the forge over https. Off by default
        [env: INK_FORGE_HOOKS]
      --forge-instances <FILE>
        A TOML file of the Forgejo instances this server
        talks to, in the format of the built-in list
        (Codeberg). A repository on a host that is on no
        list is refused before anything is sent
        [env: INK_FORGE_INSTANCES]
      --forge-gitlab <HOST>
        The GitLab hosts this server talks to; repeat or
        separate with commas. [default: gitlab.com]
        [env: INK_FORGE_GITLAB]
      --forge-route <HOST=ADDR>
        Send a forge's name to an address instead of the one
        DNS gives, as `HOST=ADDRESS:PORT`: for a mirror, or
        a test. The certificate is still checked for the
        name [env: INK_FORGE_ROUTES]
      --forge-timeout <SECONDS>
        Seconds one call to a forge may take in all.
        [default: 10] [env: INK_FORGE_TIMEOUT]
      --forge-reply <BYTES>
        The most bytes of a forge's reply that are read.
        [default: 1048576] [env: INK_FORGE_REPLY]
      --forge-body <BYTES>
        The most bytes of a webhook body that are read.
        [default: 5242880] [env: INK_FORGE_BODY]
      --forge-window <SECONDS>
        Seconds a delivery id is remembered, so the same
        delivery is taken once. [default: 600]
        [env: INK_FORGE_WINDOW]
      --forge-remembered <N>
        How many delivery ids of one repository are
        remembered at once. [default: 10000]
        [env: INK_FORGE_REMEMBERED]

Notes
  Unlike preview, this serves what `ink build` already
  wrote, through the same edge code production runs:
  conditional and range requests, the host and path
  refusals, the request and response budgets, and the
  manifest's headers as they are in production.

Exit codes
  0  Stopped with Ctrl-C
  2  The folder is no deploy, or the address cannot be used

Environment
  INK_SMTP_PASSWORD  The SMTP password for --mail smtp;
                     never a flag, so it stays out of
                     process lists. Every other sign-in
                     setting is a flag with its own INK_
                     variable

See also  ink build, ink login
Docs      https://github.com/uses-ink/uses.ink/blob/main/docs/cli.md
Global    -C, --json, -q, -v, --color, --no-progress, --log,
          --log-file (see `ink --help`)
